WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:05.105
<b>[Music]</b>

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:07.140
<b>&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Katja.</b>

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.476
<b>I'm a researcher in the Machine </b>

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:11.511
<b>Intelligence Group at MSR </b>

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:11.945
<b>Cambridge.</b>

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:14.180
<b>I'm presenting Project Malmo.</b>

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.849
<b>Project Malmo is an AI </b>

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:16.883
<b>experimentation platform that is</b>

00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:19.052
<b>open sourced and that my team </b>

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:20.754
<b>built on top of the game </b>

00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:22.222
<b>Minecraft to make it as easy as </b>

00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:23.723
<b>possible to develop and </b>

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:27.060
<b>experiment with agents that are </b>

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.428
<b>embodied within the game </b>

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:30.497
<b>Minecraft.</b>

00:00:30.497 --> 00:00:32.032
<b>We see Project Malmo as a </b>

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:33.767
<b>stepping stone toward real world</b>

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.135
<b>applications, and we're </b>

00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:35.969
<b>particularly interested in how </b>

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:38.538
<b>it can push in two specific </b>

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:39.172
<b>directions.</b>

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:41.007
<b>First, we're very interested in </b>

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:42.809
<b>this question of how agents can </b>

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.377
<b>learn to collaborate with each </b>

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:47.480
<b>other and with human players.</b>

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.350
<b>Recently we conducted the first </b>

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.619
<b>collaborative AI, Malmo </b>

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:53.820
<b>Collaborative AI Challenge, </b>

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.889
<b>where participants were tasked </b>

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:57.257
<b>to develop an agent that could </b>

00:00:57.257 --> 00:01:00.593
<b>learn to collaborate in a</b>

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:00.894
<b>pig chase task.  </b>

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:02.929
<b>So I can briefly show that here.</b>

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:05.732
<b>And this task we see two agents,</b>

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:07.767
<b>the blue and the red agent, that</b>

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:10.136
<b>are trying to catch a pig </b>

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:10.503
<b>together.</b>

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:11.938
<b>The pig is shown over here.</b>

00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:13.740
<b>We can see this either in this </b>

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.141
<b>top-down view here or in this </b>

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.210
<b>first-person view that you see </b>

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:17.777
<b>over here.</b>

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.512
<b>So I can look around and see my </b>

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:20.113
<b>collaborator here.  </b>

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:22.015
<b>And if this collaborator is able</b>

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:23.950
<b>to collaborate effectively, then</b>

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.685
<b>I can go and catch the pig right</b>

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:26.853
<b>here.</b>

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:28.922
<b>This game encapsulates some </b>

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:31.057
<b>really interesting challenges in</b>

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:34.527
<b>how we can infer intents of </b>

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:36.830
<b>other agents and how those </b>

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:39.899
<b>inferred intents or our beliefs </b>

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:40.733
<b>over their plans and their </b>

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:42.535
<b>strategy can inform the actions </b>

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:45.138
<b>that we or the AI agent should </b>

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:45.872
<b>take.</b>

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:49.209
<b>And the challenge task </b>

00:01:49.209 --> 00:01:51.911
<b>participants, graduate student </b>

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:53.346
<b>teams participated from across </b>

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:55.115
<b>the world.</b>

00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:56.649
<b>We had more than 80 teams </b>

00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:57.383
<b>participating.  </b>

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:58.818
<b>What was really interesting to </b>

00:01:58.818 --> 00:02:00.920
<b>me was they came up with widely </b>

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:01.754
<b>different approaches to this </b>

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:02.155
<b>problem.</b>

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:03.656
<b>We're currently soliciting </b>

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:04.023
<b>feedback.  </b>

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:05.158
<b>If you have any ideas on what </b>

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.192
<b>kind of tasks would be </b>

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:08.828
<b>interesting to conduct within </b>

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:10.864
<b>the Malmo platform, we would be </b>

00:02:10.864 --> 00:02:11.965
<b>very interested to hear about </b>

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:13.366
<b>that.</b>

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:16.369
<b>The Malmo platform is very open </b>

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:17.704
<b>ended and supports a wide range </b>

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:19.038
<b>of research and tasks.</b>

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:20.640
<b>If you feel like trying it out, </b>

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.243
<b>it's available on GitHub.  </b>

00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:24.611
<b>Try it out today.</b>

